    <string name="explain_title">What is this?</string>
    <string name="explain_h2_t">HTTP/2 over TLS 1.3</string>
    <string name="explain_h2_d">The tunnel is a normal HTTPS connection: TLS 1.3 handshake, then HTTP/2 frames. For a DPI box on the path it is indistinguishable from a browser talking to a website — no UDP, no custom protocol signature.</string>
    <string name="explain_mtls_t">mTLS — mutual authentication</string>
    <string name="explain_mtls_d">Both sides show certificates: the server proves it is the server, and your client proves it is yours. There is no shared password — each device has its own certificate, so losing one device does not expose the others.</string>
    <string name="explain_sni_t">SNI passthrough via relay</string>
    <string name="explain_sni_d">The Russian relay reads only the server name from the TLS hello and forwards the still-encrypted bytes to the exit node in the Netherlands. The relay cannot decrypt your traffic — it only knows where to pass it.</string>
    <string name="explain_streams_t">Stream multiplex</string>
    <string name="explain_streams_d">Traffic is split across several parallel HTTP/2 streams — as many as the CPU has cores. This raises throughput and makes the pattern on the wire look like a browser loading many resources at once.</string>
    <string name="explain_dns_t">DNS through the tunnel</string>
    <string name="explain_dns_d">Domain names are resolved by the resolver inside the tunnel, so your provider does not see which sites you open. The dot is green when a real test query succeeded, red when names stopped resolving — a tunnel that carries bytes but resolves nothing is not a working tunnel.</string>
    <string name="explain_route_t">Route</string>
    <string name="explain_route_d">Direct — the client connects to the exit node itself. Relay — traffic goes through an intermediate Russian host, which helps when the exit IP is filtered.</string>
    <string name="explain_assigned_t">Assigned address</string>
    <string name="explain_assigned_d">The IP address your device holds inside the VPN network. The server routes replies to it, and the admin panel identifies your session by it.</string>
    <string name="explain_identity_t">Identity</string>
    <string name="explain_identity_d">The client name from your certificate. The server uses it to tell devices apart, count traffic and check the subscription.</string>
    <string name="explain_sub_t">Subscription</string>
    <string name="explain_sub_d">How long this certificate stays valid. After it expires the server refuses the connection — ask the admin to extend it.</string>
